objective framing in the report."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report's primary stated goal is retirement income of £1,700 net per month, supported by the policy research document [P1] and the cashflow modelling [P10]. The recommendation of a single consolidated pension with FAD access is consistent with the stated goal of flexible retirement income and the client's age of 69 with a 6-year horizon to age 75. The Aviva Pension Portfolio supports FAD and the access mechanism is appropriate for a client already past the minimum pension age — the guard on access mechanism rather than wrapper name applies here and no access restriction concern arises.\n\nHowever, three concerns remain after the guards. First, the Kim (C2) state pension age inconsistency (66 vs 67) is an internal inconsistency flagged in the canonical model's own ambiguities and confirmed by the passages [P10]. This affects the income projection in the cashflow model and therefore the income sufficiency conclusion that underpins the recommendation. The state pension income is described as covering bills in retirement [P10], so whether it arrives at 66 or 67 materially affects the one-year gap analysis. This is a Data inconsistency that weakens the cashflow sufficiency finding.\n\nSecond, the objectives OBJ1, OBJ2 and OBJ3 are linked to recommendations throughout the report but their substantive content is only partially evidenced. The £1,700 income target appears in the policy research note [P1], which is a practitioner document rather than a client-authored or client-confirmed needs statement (category B or C). No category B or C passage sets out the objectives, priorities or time horizon in the client's own words or as confirmed by the client. The linkage of all pension-switch recommendations to OBJ2 and OBJ3 without specifying what distinguishes those objectives from each other in this context is consistent with boilerplate objective linkage rather than client-specific justification. This meets the 'Weak justification' pattern.\n\nThird, the People's Pension (EA1) exclusion from cashflow modelling inputs [P10] means the cashflow sufficiency conclusion rests on an incomplete asset base. Whether intentional or not, the report does not explain the omission, and the ambiguity noted in the canonical model is unresolved. Given that the cashflow conclusion ('no shortfall projected') is used to support the recommendation's suitability, this is a Missing component in the evidence base.\n\nThe current monthly deficit of -£288 recorded in the fact find [P24] is not addressed in the suitability report as a foreseeable financial pressure. While the report covers the investment term to age 75, it does not address how C1 manages current outgoings during the accumulation phase — a gap in the foreseeable changes analysis.\n\nVulnerability overlay: no vulnerability characteristics are recorded in the evidence (the canonical model notes /vulnerability/C1/assessment is absent from the document). No vulnerability-related adjustment is therefore required here, though the absence of a recorded vulnerability assessment is noted.\n\nOverall: the recommendation's broad direction (pension consolidation for retirement income via FAD) is consistent with the stated goal, but the objective linkage lacks client-authored evidential support, the cashflow model contains an internal inconsistency on Kim's state pension age and excludes a retained pension, and the current income deficit is unaddressed. These are material enough to constitute a Potential Concern.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"G","category":"Income/Expenditure Goal and Retirement","quote":null,"cells":["Income/ Expenditure Goal and Retirement","Needs £1,700 net per month income in retirement."]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I","category":null,"quote":"Kim will receive state pension income of £11,973 per annum from age 67.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I","category":null,"quot