{"groupId":"G2.8","requirement":"Target retirement income stated in the report is consistent with the evidence and with the figures the plan is modelled on","reportSays":"The report states the client wants to retire in 2 years with a household income of £1,700 net per month. The cashflow modelling uses annual retirement expenditure of approximately £1,700 per month as the target. Cashflow assumptions include: John earns £300/week as HGV driver, £20/week from sea cadets, Kim earns £1,100/month; John receives state pension of £11,973 p.a.; Kim receives state pension of £11,973 p.a. from age 67; expenditure inflation 2%; cashflow runs to age 100; surplus income 0% saved. The report also states Kim plans to retire at age 66 (page 3) but the cashflow assumptions use age 67 for Kim's state pension commencement (page 14). Retirement age used for pension projections is 75.","fileSays":"The fact find (P2) records: 'Thinks he'll probably retire in the next 2 years and needs a household income of £1,700 net per month.' The policy research document (P1) records: 'Needs £1,700 net per month income in retirement.' The fact find (P4) shows calculated total monthly household expenditure of £1,718.00, current net monthly income of £1,430.00, and a monthly disposable income of -£288.00. The fact find (P3) records Kim's income as £1,200/month (basic) and £230/month (state pension), totalling £1,430. The intelliflo cashflow baseline (P22, P20) runs to age 100 and shows liquid assets and pension assets over the plan. The pension switch report (P6, P7, P13) uses retirement age 75 for projections with mid growth rate 2.95%. Kim's state pension age is stated as 66 on page 3 of the report but the cashflow uses age 67.","discrepancies":["Report states Kim's income as £1,100 per month in cashflow assumptions (P24), but the fact find (P3) shows Kim's basic income as £1,200 per month.","Report states Kim plans to retire at age 66 (page 3), but cashflow assumptions state Kim's state pension commences from age 67 (P24) — internal inconsistency also reflected in the extraction ambiguities.","The report's cashflow uses annual retirement expenditure of approximately £1,700 per month as the target; the fact find records current monthly household expenditure of £1,718 (P4), which is broadly consistent but the current income is only £1,430/month, meaning there is already a pre-retirement shortfall. The report does not explicitly address how the £1,700 target will be met from pension income alone post-retirement.","The report states John's Sea Cadets income as £20 per week in the cashflow assumptions; this figure is not matched in the retrieved passages (arithmetic check flags it as unmatched)."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The core retirement income target of £1,700 net per month is well-evidenced: both the fact find [P2] and the policy research document [P1] record this figure, matching the report's assertion exactly. This is corroborated external evidence, not merely internal repetition.\n\nHowever, two material discrepancies require attention:\n\n1. Kim's employment income in the cashflow: The report states £1,100 per month [P24] but the fact find records £1,200 per month basic income [P3]. This is a data inconsistency between the report and its source document. The difference of £100/month is not trivial in a cashflow running to age 100 and affects whether the model accurately represents the household's position.\n\n2. Kim's state pension age: The report states on page 3 that Kim plans to retire at 66 and will reach state pension age at 66, but the cashflow assumptions use age 67 for Kim's state pension commencement [P24]. This internal inconsistency (also flagged in the extraction) means the cashflow modelling may understate income in the year between age 66 and 67, or alternatively the page 3 narrative misrepresents when state pension begins. Either way, the figure used in the model does not match the narrative statement of when Kim reaches state pension age, and the report does not resolve this tension.\n\n3. The £20/week sea cadets income is used in the cashflow but is not corroborated by any category B or C document in the retrieved passages. The arithmetic check also fails to match it. This is an income assumption in the model without evidential support in the file.\n\n4. The fact find shows current household expenditure of £1,718/month against current income of £1,430/month — a shortfall of £288/month before retirement. The report states the £1,700 target will be met and that the cashflow shows no shortfall, but does not explain how post-retirement income will exceed pre-retirement income given that both state pensions together (£11,973 x2 = £23,946 p.a. = ~£1,995/month combined gross) would need to cover the target net. While the cashflow tool may have modelled this correctly, the report itself does not make the linkage clear.\n\nThe Kim income discrepancy (£1,100 vs £1,200) and the Kim state pension age discrepancy (66 vs 67) are the most material findings. Neither is explained by the guards (these are not today's-money vs inflated figures, nor DB/DC base differences). The sea cadets income lack of evidence is a secondary concern. These collectively constitute a Potential Concern of moderate severity: the retirement income target itself is well evidenced, but the model inputs used to assess whether it is achievable contain inconsistencies with the source documents.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Income/ Expenditure Goal and Retirement","Needs £1,700 net per month income in retirement."]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Thinks he'll probably retire in the next 2 years and needs a household income of £1,700 net per month.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Regular Income (Today's Money)