n (category H) do provide independent corroboration of the low/mid/high projected values and the effect-of-switching amounts.\n\nHowever, several material charge-level problems remain:\n\n**Investment management charge arithmetic (Discrepancy 2):** £253.43 at 0.22% implies £115,195, not the transfer total of £110,185. The platform charge (£231.39 at 0.21%) does imply £110,186 — consistent with the transfer total — but the investment charge monetary figure does not reconcile. This is an arithmetically inconsistent presentation of charges in the report.\n\n**EA5 rows computed on wrong plan (Discrepancies 3 & 4):** The derived figures confirm that the EA5 existing annual charge (£186.19 at 0.18%) and the EA5 impact (£268.94 at 0.26%) both imply Zurich's fund value (~£103,439), not Standard Life's £3,002. The switch charges comparison for Standard Life is therefore unreliable — both the monetary figures and the impact are materially misstated. The Standard Life plan's actual ongoing charge at 0.18% on £3,002 would be approximately £5.40/year, not £186.19.\n\n**EA5 narrative contradiction (Discrepancy 5):** The page 8 narrative states the Standard Life existing plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution. The switch charges table shows +0.26% impact (new solution is more expensive). These are directly contradictory in direction. This is a finding that is not explained by any guard — it is a genuine contradiction between the narrative and the table within the same document, and it concerns the cost comparison for one of the switched plans.\n\n**Existing charge inconsistencies for Zurich and Standard Life (Discrepancies 9 & 10):** The existing arrangements table and the switch charges table give contradictory charge figures for both Zurich (0.18%+fixed vs 0.93%) and Standard Life (0.52% vs 0.18%). Given that the EA5 monetary figures appear to have been computed on Zurich's fund value rather than Standard Life's, it is plausible that the Standard Life row in the switch charges table is contaminated by Zurich's data. These are material data quality/consistency failures in the cost comparison section.\n\n**Mid-growth rate label (Discrepancy 1):** 2.94% vs 2.95% is a minor rounding difference noted in the extraction. It does not affect any comparison materially.\n\n**Investment OCF discrepancy (Discrepancy 6):** 0.22% vs 0.23% is a minor difference; the illustration's weighted average of 0.23% reflects the actual fund blend, and the guard on rounding differences applies here.\n\n**Aggregated cost illustration (Discrepancy 7):** The extraction flags this path as NotPresentInDocument. The provider illustration (P10) provides a cumulative charges table, but the suitability report does not reproduce an aggregated all-in cost figure in its own body. The check guidance identifies absence of an aggregated all-in cost as a High severity concern. However, the provider illustration is referenced in the report and does contain this information.\n\n**Vulnerability overlay:** No vulnerability characteristics are identified in the evidence that would materially alter this cost-and-charges finding.\n\nThe most serious findings are: (a) the EA5 switch charges rows being computed on Zurich's fund value rather than Standard Life's, rendering the Standard Life cost comparison unreliable; (b) the direct contradiction between the page 8 narrative and the page 11 switch charges table on whether the Standard Life switch represents a cost saving or a cost increase; (c) the investment management charge monetary figure not reconciling with the stated percentage and transfer total; and (d) the contradictory existing charge figures for Zurich and Standard Life across different sections of the report. These are material data inconsistencies and a disclosure shortfall in the cost comparison.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate - (2.95%)","Existing pension: Reduction in yield 0.3%","New solution: Reduction in yield 2.2%","Critical yield 4.8%","Effect of switching £12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Illustration - new.md","category":"H","quote":"All charges could reduce this pension fund from £129,000.00 to £115,000.00 - this is the same as reducing the investment growth after inflation from 2.9% to 0.8%. This is a reduction in the growth rate of 2.1% .","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Illustration - new.md","category":"H","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced C Acc GB00BF99W060","0.29%"]},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Illustration - new.md","category":"H","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc GB00B76WP695","0.18%"]},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Illustration - new.md","category":"H","quote":null,"cells":["Weighted Average charge","0.23%"]},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Platform Personal Pension","0.21%","0.22%","0.44%","£231.39","£253.43","£484.81"]},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Low growth rate","0.00%","£2,910"]},{"passageId":"P23","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension £127,000","New solution £125,000","Reduction in yield existing 0.3%","Reduction in yield new 0.6%","Critical yield 3.2%","Effect of switching £2,000 or 1.6% decrease"]}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Data quality","Disclosure shortfall"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

