d Retirement Flexibility claim for the recommended product. (d) The SIPP research report [P3/P12] confirms Aviva Pension Portfolio Choice was the selected product from a panel of 118, with DNA features analysis performed. (e) Zurich policy info [P9] acknowledges higher charges on the new plan are possible and asks whether the client understands their impact. (f) No independent illustration, provider charges schedule or switching analysis document is present in the file that confirms the 0.44% total ongoing charge for NP1 (implied £484.81 on c.£110,184) or reconciles the stated 4.24% cost premium figure. (g) The derived figures show EA5 existing annual charge of £186.19 at 0.18% implies a fund value matching Zurich (£103,439), not Standard Life (£3,002) — meaning the Standard Life row in the switch charges table is computed on the wrong fund value. (h) The 'Lower Costs' benefit is directly contradicted by the report's own acknowledgement that the Aviva plan is 4.24% more expensive (inclusive of adviser fees), and by the internal inconsistency where the Standard Life narrative says existing is more expensive while the charges table shows the new solution is 0.26% more expensive.","discrepancies":["The report lists 'Lower Costs' as a benefit of switching, yet separately acknowledges the Aviva plan (inclusive of adviser fees) is 4.24% more expensive than current plans, requiring £4,672 first-year outperformance — these two statements are irreconcilable as a generic benefit claim.","The Standard Life switch narrative (page 8) states the existing plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution, but the switch charges table (page 11) shows the new solution is 0.26% more expensive than the Standard Life existing plan — a direct contradiction in direction of cost differential.","EA5 (Standard Life) existing annual charge of £186.19 at 0.18% implies a fund of ~£103,439, which is Zurich's value not Standard Life's (£3,002) — the Standard Life row in the switch charges table is computed on the wrong fund value, making any cost comparison for that switch unreliable.","The five generic benefits (Lower Costs, Greater Investment Choice, Adviser Charging Facilitation, Ongoing Monitoring, Enhanced Retirement Flexibility) are stated without client-specific tailoring; none is assessed against what the ceding plans already offer, contrary to the guard requiring that a benefit the existing plan already provides cannot justify extra cost.","The report's critical yield achievability assertion — 'Based on the past performance of your recommended funds, I do believe that this is achievable' — is unsupported by any performance evidence in the file specific to the recommended Aviva portfolio in its recommended fund mix.","The aggregated all-in cost illustration (path /costsAndCharges/aggregatedCostIllustration) is absent from the report (confirmed NotPresentInDocument), so the client cannot see the cumulative cost impact of the recommendation.","The 'Lower Costs' benefit is stated for the Aviva switch covering EA3 (Aviva existing), yet the report acknowledges overall the plan is more expensive — no net saving is evidenced for any individual switch.","Standard Life already offers FAD per [P19], so the 'Enhanced Retirement Flexibility' benefit does not differentiate the recommended product from Standard Life's existing arrangement."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The central problem is that the report simultaneously claims 'Lower Costs' as a justification for switching and acknowledges the recommended Aviva plan is 4.24% more expensive (inclusive of adviser fees) than the current arrangements. These two positions cannot coexist as a coherent justification. The 'Lower Costs' benefit claim is therefore unsupported and contradicted by the report's own data. The guard that consolidation convenience is a real but non-financial benefit applies to some of the other stated benefits (Adviser Charging Facilitation, Ongoing Monitoring), but it does not rescue the 'Lower Costs' claim, which is a financial assertion. The Standard Life cost comparison is doubly problematic: the narrative direction contradicts the charges table, and the derived figures confirm the table row was computed using Zurich's fund value rather than Standard Life's £3,002 — meaning the 0.18% existing charge figure for EA5 is arithmetically impossible against Standard Life's own fund value (£3,002 × 0.18% = ~£5.40, not £186.19). Any cost justification resting on that row is unreliable. The 'Enhanced Retirement Flexibility' benefit has partial support: Zurich (P19) offers only UFPLS and lacks FAD, so moving to Aviva does genuinely add FAD for that ceding plan. However, Standard Life already offers FAD per [P19], so for the Standard Life switch this benefit does not differentiate. The benefits are stated generically and are not assessed against each individual ceding plan's features, as the guard requires. The critical yield achievability assertion relies on past performance of the recommended funds, but no performance data specific to the recommended Aviva portfolio in its HSBC/CT fund mix is produced in the file — the research report contains fund research but no historical performance evidence calibrated to the specific 4.8% per annum growth requirement cited. The absence of an aggregated cost illustration means the client cannot assess the cumulative cost impact. The 0.01% rounding difference in mid-growth rate (2.94% narrative vs 2.95% appendix) is a minor internal inconsistency noted but not material to this check. The vulnerability overlay: no formal vulnerability assessment is recorded in the file (/vulnerability/C1/assessment NotPresentInDocument), but no specific vulnerability driver is identified that would alter this cost and charges finding. Overall: the 'Lower Costs' benefit claim is directly contradicted by the report's own cost data; the Standard Life cost comparison is computed on the wrong fund value; the generic benefit statements are not assessed against existing plan features; and the critical yield achievability assertion is unsupported by evidence. These are material concerns.","citations":[{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs — The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"the Aviva plan is 4.24% more expensive than your current plans. This means that your proposed plan will need to outperform your current plan by £4,672 in the first year (in monetary terms)","cells":null},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - D2301334000","FAD","300","Yes",""]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Total aggregated fund costs:","£0.00 (0.00%)","£258.93 (0.24%)","£147.80 (0.13%)","£0.00 (0.00%)","£406.74 (0.37%)"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"To give you the control and flexibility to manage your benefits in retirement. You have several options: take all of your pension pot as a cash lump sum. Up to 25% will be paid tax free, but you'll pay tax at your marginal rate on the rest. take up to 25% of your pension pot tax free as a lump sum and/or as a regular income, and then take the remaining pension pot as and when you need it through income drawdown whilst continuing to manage the investments within your pension pot.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan. Based on the past per