evant to the recommendation. The pension switch research report [P13] and SIPP research report [P8] contain no independent tax consequence statements. No document in the file contradicts the report's tax disclosures. No client-specific tax band or income tax rate is evidenced in the file for the purpose of verifying marginal-rate statements.","discrepancies":["The report states death benefits before age 75 are 'paid free of tax' without qualification; the Key Features [P4] qualifies this — if the value of tax-free benefits taken from pension plans during lifetime and on death exceeds the lump sum & death benefit allowance, the beneficiary may pay income tax on the excess. The report does not mention this qualification.","The report's FAD section [P6] states unused pension fund can 'usually be passed down to a wide range of beneficiaries free from Inheritance Tax' but simultaneously the report on page 9 warns that from 6 April 2027 DC pensions will be included in the taxable estate for IHT. These two statements in the same report are in tension, though the 2027 change arguably resolves the 'usually' qualifier. No external evidence contradicts either statement.","The mid growth rate is stated as 2.94% in the page 11 narrative and 2.95% in the page 18 appendix table — a minor internal inconsistency already flagged in the extraction metadata; no tax consequence flows from this.","No client-specific marginal income tax rate is stated or evidenced, making the marginal-rate tax statements unverifiable in respect of their precise impact on this client — though per the guard this is unverifiable, not necessarily wrong.","The lump sum & death benefit allowance (£1,073,100) is mentioned in the FAD/Lifetime Allowance section [P6] but the death benefits tax treatment on page 9 references only the pre-75 and post-75 outcomes without mentioning the lump sum & death benefit allowance threshold that could cause a tax charge even on pre-75 deaths."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report contains a reasonably comprehensive set of tax disclosures covering the main consequences of the recommendation: contribution tax relief, tax-free cash at crystallisation, drawdown income taxation, death benefit tax treatment, small pot encashment tax treatment (with a client-specific monetary illustration), the 2027 IHT change, and the Annual Allowance. These are genuine disclosures rather than delegation to product literature, and the small pot calculation is client-specific and numerically illustrated.\n\nThe most material discrepancy is the treatment of pre-75 death benefits. The report states beneficiaries receive benefits 'free of tax' without qualification. The Key Features document [P4] makes clear that this is only true up to the lump sum & death benefit allowance (£1,073,100); above that, the beneficiary may pay income tax on the excess. The report does mention the lump sum & death benefit allowance of £268,275 in the context of tax-free cash, and mentions the lump sum & death benefit allowance of £1,073,100 in the Lifetime Allowance section [P6], but does not connect it to the death benefit tax treatment on page 9. Given the combined pension values are well below £1,073,100, this omission is unlikely to be material to this particular client in practice; however, the report's unqualified statement that pre-75 death benefits are 'paid free of tax' is not fully consistent with [P4].\n\nThe tension between the FAD section's statement that unused pension can 'usually be passed down free from Inheritance Tax' and the page 9 warning about the 2027 IHT change is present but not a discrepancy — the qualifier 'usually' and the 2027 change are both stated in the report, and the report clearly draws the client's attention to the forthcoming change. The £0 IHT liability statement [P3] is consistent with the assets disclosed.\n\nThe absence of the client's specific marginal income tax rate in the file means the marginal-rate statements cannot be verified as to their precise impact, but per the guard this makes them unverifiable rather than wrong.\n\nThe internal inconsistency between 2.94% and 2.95% mid growth rate has no tax consequence.\n\nThe Standard Life cost direction contradiction (page 8 narrative vs page 11 table) identified in the extraction metadata does not affect tax disclosures.\n\nOverall, the tax disclosure is substantive and covers the main consequences. The one genuine gap is the failure to mention the lump sum & death benefit allowance threshold in the context of pre-75 death benefit tax treatment, which creates a partial inconsistency with [P4]. Given the fund values involved, this is a low-severity disclosure shortfall rather than a material omission.","citations":[{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"An individual can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275 for people with no form of lifetime allowance protection).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Currently, your Inheritance Tax liability will be £0.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"If you die before you're 75, we'll normally pay any benefits tax free to your nominated beneficiaries, at the trustees' discretion (unless you have an Aviva Pension Trust, in which case the rules of that Trust will apply). If the value of tax-free benefits taken from your pension plan(s) during your lifetime and on death is more than the lump sum & death benefit allowance, the beneficiary may pay income tax on the excess.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"If you die after the age of 75 we can pay the full value of your remaining pension fund to your nominated beneficiaries, at the discretion of the trustees. Any benefits will be taxed at your beneficiaries' marginal rate.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"You can normally withdraw up to 25% of your pension fund as a tax-free lump sum. You may have to pay income tax on any income payments and lump sum payments you take.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Under the small pot rules, a £200 pension fund withdrawal would be taxed with 25% taken as tax-free cash and the remaining 75% taxed as income at your marginal tax rate. We estimate you would receive £170 net.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"from 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Disclosure shortfall"],"severity":"Low","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

