rative states 2.94% — minor but a documented internal inconsistency.","Performance chart captions in the suitability report (P15) assign letters A=Aviva and D=Zurich for performance, but the volatility chart caption swaps these assignments — creating ambiguity about which fund's volatility data is being presented to the client."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The requirement is to assess whether alternatives to the recommended switches were considered and properly justified, and whether those justifications are supported by evidence.\n\nThe report considers a range of alternatives — do nothing, in-situ fund switch (per ceding plan), consolidate into employer scheme, stakeholder pension, investment pathways — and provides plan-specific reasons for rejection. This is a materially complete alternatives framework.\n\nHowever, there are two substantive concerns:\n\n1. Standard Life cost justification is contradicted and computed on the wrong base. The report states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution', which is offered as a reason to switch. The pension switch charges table at page 11 shows the impact as +0.26% — in the opposite direction (new solution is more expensive). The extraction flags this as a direct contradiction. Furthermore, the derived figures confirm that the EA5 charge row is calculated using Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's own value (£3,002), making the entire Standard Life cost comparison in the switch table arithmetically unreliable. This means the cost-saving rationale for switching Standard Life (the smallest plan at £3,002) is unsupported and internally contradicted. The other reasons given (lifestyling, adviser servicing, investment choice) are client-specific and plausible, but the lead justification — lower cost — is contradicted by the report's own data.\n\n2. The overall switch produces materially lower projected values. The consolidated comparison at mid-growth shows £115,000 (new) vs £127,000 (existing), a £12,000 or 9.4% decrease. The Zurich-only comparison shows £125,000 vs £127,000. The report does address that the switch results in higher charges (the critical yield analysis and effect-of-switching rows make this plain), and the qualitative benefits — adviser servicing, fund consolidation, drawdown access, diversification — are genuine and client-specific. The Zurich policy document (P9) confirms no safeguarded benefits, guaranteed annuity rates, or GMP apply, so no valuable features are being lost on the Zurich transfer. The switch report states 'No benefits were listed for the existing products' for all three plans. These qualitative justifications are supported by category E and G evidence. The consolidation rationale is a valid client objective (the client wanted to consolidate pensions — see CHK-002 guard). So the projected value shortfall, while material, is addressed by the qualitative case.\n\nThe dominant concern is the Standard Life cost contradiction. The plan is small (£3,002) so the practical impact on the client is limited, but the report's cost rationale for switching it is directly contradicted by the switch report's own charges table, and the underlying arithmetic is wrong because the charge was computed on Zurich's fund value. This is a Data inconsistency that also weakens the justification for that element of the recommendation.\n\nThe volatility chart label swap (P15) and the 2.94%/2.95% discrepancy are minor presentation issues and do not affect the substance of the alternatives analysis.\n\nOption 3 (consolidate into an existing pension) is addressed only via the employer scheme rejection. No analysis is presented of whether the Zurich plan (by far the largest at £103,439) could have served as a consolidation vehicle for the other two. This is a missing component in the alternatives analysis, though a moderate one given that the Zurich plan itself does not allow adviser servicing and has limited fund options, reasons which implicitly apply.\n\nOverall: the alternatives framework is substantive and mostly supported by evidence, but the Standard Life cost argument is materially contradicted by the report's own data, and the Option 3 in-situ consolidation analysis is incomplete. These are Potential Concerns at moderate severity — the Standard Life plan is small and other reasons for switching it exist, but the contradiction is genuine and the missing Option 3 analysis for consolidation into an existing plan is a gap.","citations":[{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension projected value at age 75","£127,000","New solution projected value at age 75","£115,000","Effect of switching","£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Zurich - Zurich pension only","Mid growth rate","Existing pension","£127,000","New solution","£125,000","Effect of switching","£2,000 or 1.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"No benefits were listed for the existing products","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Guarantees, bonuses, guaranteed minimum pension, safeguarding, life styling, block/bulk transfers, market value reduction, protected or enhanced tax free cash do not apply to this plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - SL pension only","Mid growth rate – (2.94%)","Existing pension projected value","£3,440","New solution projected value","£3,420","Effect of switching","£20 or 0.6% decrease"]},{"pass