{"groupId":"G9.10","requirement":"Material replacement-related information omitted from the recommendation — safeguarded benefits, exit penalties, employer contributions lost, and other valuable features that may be lost on switching.","reportSays":"The suitability report asserts for all three ceding plans (Aviva EA3, Zurich EA4, Standard Life EA5): (1) no safeguarded benefits exist ('This pension arrangement does not contain any safeguarded benefits'); (2) no exit penalties apply ('No charges for transfer and/or switching'); (3) no employer contributions will be lost (employerContributionsLost = false for all). The extraction notes that featuresOrGuaranteesLost paths for all three plans are absent from the document (NotPresentInDocument), confirming the report makes no disclosure of any features or guarantees that would be lost beyond the generic 'no safeguarded benefits' statement.","fileSays":"[P11] Zurich policy info discloses that if the plan has capital units an exit charge applies on transfer, reducing by ~0.3–0.4% per month until SRA, capped at 1% from age 55. [P13] Zurich confirms 'There are no deductions taken from the current value if benefits are transferred or taken. The initial set up charges have already been paid' — but this applies only where there are no capital units; the capital-units caveat in [P11] is unresolved. [P11] also discloses that Zurich does not offer partial UFPLS (only a full UFPLS if one has been taken previously) and cannot take just the 25% tax-free lump sum leaving the remainder for later. [P16] Standard Life confirms no bid-offer spread, 100% allocation, no exit charge, no protected tax-free cash (standard 25% only). [P9] confirms Standard Life value £3,002.02. [P1]–[P8] and [P10] show the People's Pension (EA1, retained) has ongoing employer and employee contributions — the retained plan is not switched, so employer contributions on EA1 are not at risk from this transaction. The Zurich policy ([P11]) shows no current regular payments (£0.00 employer, £0.00 employee), confirming the scheme is not live/active; the historic payments were all protected-rights transfers/former protected rights. [P20] policy research file shows no MVR for any plan. [P22] critical yield report (category G) shows the effect of switching is negative across all growth scenarios (e.g. mid-rate: £12,000 / 9.4% decrease) but is presented as a comparison tool rather than contradicting the switch. [P19] KIID shows an ongoing charge of 0.18% for the recommended fund. No provider documentation in the file positively confirms 'no safeguarded benefits' for Zurich or Aviva.","discrepancies":["The report asserts no exit penalty for Zurich (EA4) ('No charges for transfer and/or switching'), but [P11] discloses a capital-units exit charge that reduces by ~0.3–0.4% per month and is capped at 1% from age 55 — whether this applies to EA4 is unresolved by the file, and the report makes no reference to it.","The report makes no disclosure of any features or guarantees lost beyond the generic 'no safeguarded benefits' statement; [P11] discloses that Zurich cannot offer partial UFPLS or a standalone 25% tax-free lump sum, which are retirement-flexibility features that would be lost on transfer and are not mentioned in the report.","The extraction confirms featuresOrGuaranteesLost is NotPresentInDocument for all three ceding plans, meaning the report contains no plan-specific disclosure of retirement-option features foregone on switching Zurich.","The report asserts no safeguarded benefits for all ceding plans, but no provider documentation corroborates this for Zurich or Aviva — the Standard Life file [P16] is the only one that positively addresses protected tax-free cash ('no Protected Tax Free Cash and the standard 25% will be available').","Internal inconsistency in the charges used for Standard Life (EA5): the existing arrangements table states 0.52% fund charge, but the switch charges table states 0.18% current annual recurring charge, and the derived figures show the EA5 annual charge of £186.19 at 0.18% is computed on a fund value consistent with Zurich (£103,439) not Standard Life (£3,002) — meaning the cost/benefit comparison for EA5 rests on a materially wrong base value.","Internal inconsistency in the charges used for Zurich (EA4): existing arrangements table states 0.18% + £2.12/month admin, but the switch charges table states 0.93% current annual recurring charge — these cannot both be correct as a like-for-like total.","The critical yield report [P22] shows the effect of switching is negative at every growth rate (mid: −£12,000/−9.4%; high: −£15,000/−10.0%), yet the report does not prominently address why switching is justified given this outcome — though this is partly addressed by the consolidation rationale."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"Several concerns arise from this requirement. First and most material: the Zurich policy information [P11] discloses a capital-units exit charge (reducing ~0.3–0.4%/month, capped at 1% from age 55) that may apply on transfer. The report flatly states 'No charges for transfer and/or switching' for EA4 (Zurich). [P13] does contain a transfer value and early retirement charge table stating 'There are no deductions taken from the current value if benefits are transferred' — but this is qualified by [P11]'s capital-units caveat. Whether EA4 holds capital units is not confirmed in the file, so the capital-units exit charge cannot be ruled out. The report makes no acknowledgement of this contingency whatsoever. This is a Disclosure shortfall and a potential Missing component. Second: [P11] discloses that Zurich does not offer partial UFPLS and cannot isolate the 25% tax-free lump sum. These are valuable retirement-flexibility features that are specific to the Zurich contract. The report records 'NotPresentInDocument' for featuresOrGuaranteesLost, confirming the report makes no disclosure of these foregone features. This is a further Disclosure shortfall. Third: the report asserts no safeguarded benefits for all three plans but no category-E or category-G document corroborates this for Zurich (EA4) or Aviva (EA3). Standard Life [P16] does confirm no protected tax-free cash. Absence of corroborating provider documentation for Zurich and Aviva means these assertions are unsupported, not confirmed. Fourth: the charge arithmetic identified in the derived figures shows that the EA5 (Standard Life) cost comparison is computed using a fund value matching Zurich (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's actual value (~£3,002), making the switch charges table internally unreliable for EA5. Combined with the contradictory charge figures for both Zurich and Standard Life already flagged in the extraction's internalInconsistencies, the cost/benefit analysis for these plans contains material data quality issues. The employer contributions point: Zurich [P11] shows £0.00 current employer payments and no regular payments, confirming no active employer contributions are at risk from the Zurich transfer. The People's Pension is retained, so its employer contributions (evidenced in [P1]–[P8]) are not affected. The employer contributions assertions (false) are therefore consistent with the file evidence. The vulnerability overlay: no vulnerability assessment is present in the canonical model (NotPresentInDocument for /vulnerability/C1/assessment) so no vulnerability-specific overlay adjustment can be made. Overall, the most significant issues are: (a) the capital-units exit charge for Zurich is potentially applicable but completely unaddressed; (b) Zurich-specific retirement-flexibility features (no partial UFPLS, no standalone 25% drawdown) that would be lost on transfer are not disclosed; (c) the safeguarded-benefits assertions for Zurich and Aviva are unsupported by any provider documentation; and (d) the Standard Life and Zurich charge comparisons contain material internal inconsistencies that undermine the reliability of the cost/benefit analysis. These collectively constitute a Potential Concern at High severity, primarily because the largest ceding plan (Zurich, £103,439) has an unresolved exit charge risk and undisclosed retirement-option losses, and the charge comparison table for both EA4 and EA5 is internally inconsistent.","citations":[{"passageId":"P11","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"If this plan has capital units, there will be an exit charge applied if the fund is transferred. This exit charge is a standard term of the contract. The charge is taken in order to recover the charges, which would be due, if the fund were to remain invested with Zurich until the customer has reached their selected retirement age (SRA). The charge is taken by reducing the number of capital units paid out; the charge reduces by around 0.3%-0.4% a month until SRA where there is no charge. The charge is capped at 1% from age 55.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Zurich does not currently offer the facility to take just the 25% tax-free lump sum from our pension plans, leaving the remainder to claim at a later date.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"We do not currently offer the option to take a partial UFPLS.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"There are no deductions taken from the current value if benefits are transferred or taken. The initial set up charges have already been paid.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"Standard_Life_Policy_Info.md","category":"E","quote":"For these contracts, if there was no block transfer, then there is definitely no Protected Tax Free Cash and the standard 25% will be available.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"Standard_Life_Policy_Info.md","category":"E","quote":"No bid offer spread.\n100% allocation.\nNo loyalty bonus.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Market Value Reduction (MVR)?","No","No","n/a","n/a","No","No"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Projected value at age 75","£127,000","£115,000"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching","–","£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":null,"cells":["Personal payments : Regular payments £0.00, Single payments £0.00","Employer payments : Regular payments £0.00, Single payments £0.00"]}],"issueCategories":["Disclosure shortfall","Missing component","Data inconsistency"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

