arison shows projected values at mid growth: existing £127,000 vs new solution £115,000 — a £12,000 / 9.4% decrease [P7]. At low growth: existing £108,000 vs new £97,600 — £10,400 / 9.6% decrease [P7]. At high growth: existing £150,000 vs new £135,000 — £15,000 / 10% decrease [P7]. Critical yields: 1.9% (low), 4.8% (mid), 7.8% (high) [P7]. Standard Life only comparison: low growth existing £2,910 vs new £2,900; critical yield 6.0% [P6, P5]. Aviva only: reduction in yield 0.6% both sides; critical yield 5.9% high growth [P8]. Immediate impact: existing pension £110,185.60, new solution day one £106,880.60 — shortfall of £3,305 [P2, P7]. The switch report does not contain the specific charge figures cited in the suitability report (−0.06%, −£2.25, −0.49%, −£506.85, +0.26%, +£268.94, 0.18% Standard Life existing, 0.93% Zurich existing). The Defaqto report confirms independent switch modelling exists but the Zurich comparison shows the new solution underperforms the existing plan at every growth rate, with the new solution RIY materially higher (0.3% existing vs 2.1-2.3% new).","discrepancies":["1. Standard Life EA5 narrative (page 8) states existing plan is 0.08% more expensive than recommended; switch charges table (page 11) shows new solution is 0.26% MORE expensive than existing — direction of cost comparison is contradicted within the report itself.","2. EA5 existing annual charge: £186.19 at 0.18% arithmetically implies a fund of ~£103,439 (Zurich's value), not Standard Life's £3,002 — the EA5 cost row is computed on the wrong plan's fund value, rendering the comparison unreliable.","3. EA5 impact of £268.94 at 0.26% likewise implies Zurich's fund value (~£103,439), not Standard Life's £3,002 — same wrong-fund-value error.","4. Zurich existing charge shown as 0.18% + £2.12/month on page 5 but 0.93% total annual recurring charge in the switch charges table on page 11 — materially inconsistent and unreconciled.","5. Standard Life existing charge shown as 0.52% on page 5 but 0.18% in the switch charges table on page 11 — directly contradictory.","6. The Defaqto switch report shows the new solution underperforms the existing Zurich plan at every growth rate scenario (e.g. mid growth: existing £127,000 vs new £115,000, a £12,000 / 9.4% decrease), with new solution RIY of 2.1-2.3% against existing 0.3% — a material cost increase that the suitability report's charge table claims is a saving of -0.49%.","7. The suitability report's charge figures (−0.06%, −£2.25, −0.49%, −£506.85, +0.26%, +£268.94) are not corroborated by any passage in the evidence file; none of these specific values appear in the retrieved G-category documents.","8. Immediate fund value impact: existing £110,185.60 vs new solution day-one £106,880.60 — a £3,305 shortfall disclosed in the switch report [P2/P7] but not addressed in the suitability report's cost/benefit analysis.","9. The report's own figures show EA5 (Standard Life, £3,002) is being switched to a plan that costs 0.26% more per annum (per the table), yet the narrative states it is cheaper — a direct internal contradiction that is not resolved.","10. Critical yield for the Zurich plan at mid growth is 4.8%, which exceeds the mid growth rate of 2.95% stated in the report, meaning the new solution is projected to underperform the existing Zurich plan under the mid growth scenario — a material finding the report does not adequately address."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"This requirement fails on multiple, compounding grounds. First, there is a direct internal contradiction in the Standard Life (EA5) section: the page 8 narrative claims the existing plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution, but the switch charges table on page 11 shows the new solution is 0.26% more expensive. These cannot both be correct and the directional error means the cost justification for switching Standard Life is unreliable. Second, the derived figures confirm that the EA5 cost rows in the switch charges table are arithmetically computed on Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's £3,002 — the comparison is therefore built on the wrong denominator and is not a valid like-for-like analysis for Standard Life. Third, the Zurich charge figures are materially inconsistent between the existing arrangements table (0.18% + £2.12/month) and the switch charges table (0.93%), and Standard Life's charges are contradicted between 0.52% (page 5) and 0.18% (page 11). These inconsistencies mean the charge comparison cannot be relied upon. Fourth, and most materially, the independent Defaqto switch report (G-category evidence) shows that for the Zurich plan — by far the largest ceding plan at £103,439 — the new solution underperforms at every growth rate scenario, with RIY of 2.1–2.3% against the existing 0.3%, producing projected shortfalls of £10,400 to £15,000. The critical yield at mid growth (4.8%) exceeds the assumed mid growth rate (2.95%), meaning outperformance is not projected to be achieved. The suitability report asserts a -0.49% cost saving for the Zurich switch, which directly contradicts the Defaqto evidence showing the new solution is substantially more expensive in terms of reduction in yield. The non-financial benefits stated (investment choice, adviser charging, monitoring, lifestyling risk) are not client-specific for the most part and do not include a quantified or evidenced analysis of why they justify a material cost increase for Zurich. The immediate impact of a £3,305 day-one reduction in fund value is noted in the switch report but not addressed in the suitability report. No vulnerability factors are identified in the evidence that would further aggravate or mitigate these findings. The non-financial benefits guard applies to some qualitative benefits but cannot excuse the direct contradiction in cost direction for Standard Life or the absence of corroboration for the Zurich cost saving claim, which is contradicted by the independent evidence. This is a high-severity concern: the largest ceding plan (Zurich) has a cost/benefit analysis that is contradicted by the independent switch report, and the Standard Life analysis contains an internal directional error compounded by a wrong-fund-value arithmetic error.","citations":[{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension projected value at age 75","£127,000","New solution projected value at age 75","£115,000","Critical yield","4.8%","Effect of switching","-£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Low growth rate – (0.00%)","Existing pension projected value","£108,000","New solution projected value","£97,600","Reduction in yield existing","0.3%","Reduction in yield new","2.1%","Critical yield","1.9%","Effect of switching","-£10,400 or 9.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["High growth rate – (5.88%)","Existing pension projected value","£150,000","New solution projected value","£135,000","Reduction in yield existing","0.3%","Reduction in yield new","2.3%","Critical yield","7.8%","Effect of switching","-£15,000 or 10.0% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension:","£110,185.60","New solution day one:","£106,880.60"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - SL pension only","Low growth rate","Existing pension projected value at age 75","£2,910","New solution projected value at age 75","£2,900","Critical yield","6.0%"]},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Reduction in yield existing","0.6%","Reduction in yield new","0.6%","Critical yield","6.