{"groupId":"G9.8","requirement":"Evidence supporting claims that higher costs are justified by additional benefits","reportSays":"The report makes plan-specific cost/benefit justifications for three switches. For Aviva (EA3): lower costs (existing plan 0.06% more expensive than recommended), lifestyling risk, greater investment choice, adviser charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring. Net benefit conclusion: lower costs, greater investment flexibility, continued professional oversight. For Zurich (EA4): greater investment choice, multi-asset fund alignment (Zurich cannot offer risk level 5 in a multi-asset fund; blend of risk 7 and risk 2 used), enhanced retirement flexibility, adviser charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring. Net benefit conclusion: greater investment flexibility, improved retirement options, continued professional oversight. For Standard Life (EA5): lower costs (plan itself 0.08% more expensive than recommended), lifestyling risk, greater investment choice, enhanced retirement flexibility, adviser charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring. Net benefit conclusion: lower costs, greater investment flexibility, improved retirement options, continued professional oversight. The switch charges table (page 11) shows: Aviva -0.06% / -£2.25 (saving); Zurich -0.49% / -£506.85 (saving); Standard Life +0.26% / +£268.94 (new plan MORE expensive). The critical yield narrative (page 11/18) states mid-growth rate of 2.94%/2.95% and that the plan needs to grow at 4.8% p.a. to match the existing plan. The report asserts this is achievable based on past fund performance.","fileSays":"[P13] (Pension switch report summary) confirms the three plans being switched (Aviva, Zurich, Standard Life) to an Aviva SIPP. [P14] (suitability report, switch charges table) shows Standard Life existing annual recurring charge 0.18%, impact of recommended alternative +0.26%, +£268.94 — the new plan is more expensive for Standard Life. [P15] (suitability report, Standard Life recommendation section) states 'Lower Costs — The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution', which directionally contradicts the switch charges table. [P4] (Pension switch report, Zurich-only alternative comparison) shows that switching Zurich alone produces a negative effect at all growth rates (e.g. mid growth: existing £127,000 vs new solution £115,000; effect of switching -£12,000 or -9.4% decrease; critical yield 4.8%). [P8] (Pension switch report, new solution comparison ranking) shows the new solution ranks 5th out of top 10 (£125,000 projected maturity value at mid growth), behind the existing solution (2nd, £127,000), Zurich alone (1st, £128,000), Aviva alone (3rd, £126,000) and Standard Life alone (4th, £126,000). [P3] (Pension switch report, alternative comparisons) shows that retaining Zurich and switching only Aviva/Standard Life produces lower projected maturity than retaining all. [P12] (Pension switch report appendix) notes the client's agreed risk level is 5 and that Zurich fund data shows as N/A. [P5] (Zurich policy info) confirms fund switch charge £0.00 and no transfer value deduction. [P9] (Zurich policy info, transfer considerations checklist) flags questions about guaranteed annuity rates, fund choices and charge comparisons but the completed checkboxes are not shown. [P2] (Pension switch report) confirms immediate day-one shortfall of £3,305 (existing £110,185.60 vs new solution day-one £106,880.60). [P18] (suitability report, critical yield narrative) states mid-growth rate 2.94% and required growth of 4.8%. [P1] (Pension switch report) documents the overall switching analysis framework.","discrepancies":["Standard Life (EA5) cost direction contradiction: the recommendation narrative (page 8/[P15]) states the existing plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution (i.e. a saving), but the switch charges table (page 11/[P14]) shows the impact of the recommended alternative as +0.26% / +£268.94 — meaning the new plan is MORE expensive than Standard Life by £268.94 per year. The report's stated justification of 'lower costs' for Standard Life is directly contradicted by the report's own switch charges table.","EA5 charge arithmetic confirms wrong-plan calculation: derived figures show that EA5's existing annual charge of £186.19 at 0.18% and the £268.94 impact at 0.26% both imply a fund value of ~£103,438 (Zurich's value), not Standard Life's value of £3,002. The Standard Life cost comparison in the switch charges table appears to have been computed on Zurich's fund value, making it unreliable.","The new solution ranks 5th in the Defaqto comparison (mid-growth £125,000), below the existing solution (2nd, £127,000) and all three individual existing plans retained (Aviva 3rd £126,000, Standard Life 4th £126,000, Zurich 1st £128,000), yet the report does not address why a solution delivering lower projected maturity is in the client's best interests.","For Zurich (EA4), the pension switch report shows the effect of switching at mid growth is -£12,000 / -9.4% decrease with a critical yield of 4.8%. The report asserts this is achievable based on past performance but provides no specific fund performance data or evidence that 4.8% growth is reliably achievable, particularly given Zurich's fund data in the switch report shows as N/A.","The report claims 'Lower Costs' as the first justification for the Standard Life switch in the net benefit conclusion, but the switch charges table shows a cost increase of £268.94 p.a. The net benefit conclusion for Standard Life says 'lower costs' — a claim that is false per the report's own table.","The report claims lifestyling as a reason for switching Standard Life but no evidence in the file (category E or G) confirms Standard Life's fund is a lifestyling fund.","The report claims 'Greater Investment Choice' as a benefit for all three switches but no fund count comparison between existing and recommended plans is provided in the file to quantify this claim.","Mid-growth rate stated as 2.94% in narrative [P18] but appendix table [P17] labels it 2.95% — a minor internal inconsistency.","Zurich existing arrangement charge stated as 0.18% fund + £2.12/month on page 5 of the suitability report, but the switch charges table (page 11/[P14]) states 0.93% as the current annual recurring charge — these are materially inconsistent, undermining the reliability of the Zurich cost-saving claim of -0.49%."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"This requirement asks whether claims that higher costs are justified by additional benefits are supported by evidence. The guard specifies that where the recommended plan is cheaper, this requirement does not bite — but it bites squarely where it is more expensive.\n\nStandard Life (EA5) is the clearest and most serious problem. The report's own switch charges table ([P14]) shows the new plan is +0.26% / +£268.94 per year MORE expensive than Standard Life. Yet the recommendation narrative ([P15]) claims 'The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' — a directly contradictory directional claim. The net benefit conclusion also states 'lower costs' as a benefit of switching Standard Life. The derived figures confirm that the EA5 charge rows in the switch charges table were computed on Zurich's fund value (~£103,438), not Standard Life's fund value (£3,002), so the entire cost comparison for EA5 is unreliable. The report does not address or justify the cost increase for Standard Life with any specific benefit analysis beyond generic claims (greater investment choice, adviser charging facilitation, lifestyling) that are not individually evidenced for this plan. No category E or G document provides plan-specific evidence supporting these claims for Standard Life.\n\nFor Zurich (EA4), the switch charges table shows a saving of -0.49% / -£506.85 at the plan level, so the 'cheaper' guard partially applies. However, the independent Defaqto pension switch report ([P4], [P8]) shows the new solution produces lower projected maturity values than retaining Zurich alone at every growth rate (mid: -£12,000 / -9.4% decrease), and the new solution ranks 5th overall while Zurich alone ranks 1st. The report addresses this through the critical yield narrative, asserting 4.8% growth is achievable based on past fund performance, but provides no specific performance evidence — the Zurich fund data in the switch report ([P12]) shows as N/A. The justification that multi-asset fund alignment and better fund performance outweigh the projected maturity shortfall is made by assertion, without corroborating evidence from categories E or G.\n\nFor Aviva (EA3), the switch shows a saving of -0.06% / -£2.25, so cost is lower and this limb does not bite materially for EA3.\n\nThe inconsistency between the Zurich charges on page 5 (0.18% + £2.12/month) and the switch charges table (0.93%) is flagged as an internal inconsistency that undermines the reliability of the Zurich cost-saving figure.\n\nOn vulnerability: the extraction notes no vulnerability assessment was present in the document (/vulnerability/C1/assessment NotPresentInDocument). No vulnerability factors are identified in the retrieved passages that would materially alter this finding.\n\nIn summary: the Standard Life switch is justified on 'lower costs' when the report's own table shows higher costs, the cost calculation for Standard Life appears to have used the wrong fund value, and the benefits claimed are not corroborated by any category E or G evidence. The Zurich switch relies on a critical yield of 4.8% being achievable with no evidential support. These are material concerns.","citations":[{"passageId":"P14","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","£186.19","0.26%","£268.94"]},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges. The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"By switching to this scheme, you will benefit from lower costs, greater investment flexibility, improved retirement options, and continued professional oversight, ensuring your pension remains on track to support your long-term financial goals.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Projected value at age 75","£127,000","£115,000"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching","–","£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Critical yield","–","4.8%"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["1","Product: Zurich - Zurich pension Platform: None","0.2%","£128,000"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["2","Existing Solution","0.3%","£127,000"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["5","New Solution","0.6%","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan. Based on the past performance of your recommended funds, I do believe that this is achievable.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Fund","Pro