cted maturity values at all growth rates — yet the report claims lower costs as a primary benefit without adequately reconciling this.","6. Immediate death benefit shortfall of £3,305 disclosed in the switch report (P7/P12) is not referenced in the suitability report's costs of switching section.","7. Zurich existing charge: existing arrangements table states 0.18% fund charge plus £2.12/month admin, but the switch charges table states 0.93% as the current annual recurring charge for Zurich — these are materially inconsistent and cannot both be correct as a total.","8. Standard Life existing charge: existing arrangements table states 0.52% fund charge, but the switch charges table states 0.18% as the current annual recurring charge — directly contradictory.","9. Scottish Widows (EA2, retained) policy note warns that transfer may result in loss of protected tax-free cash or protected pension age — though EA2 is stated as retained, this note highlights the sensitivity around guarantees that is not addressed for the switching plans.","10. The report does not address whether benefits outweigh the higher cost for Standard Life (EA5), given the switch charges table shows it is more expensive after switching."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"Several material problems affect this requirement. The most significant is the Standard Life (EA5) cost direction contradiction: the suitability report on page 8 tells the client the existing plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution, implying a saving from switching, while the switch charges table on page 11 — within the same report — shows the new solution costs 0.26%/£268.94 more per year. These are opposite directional claims and cannot both be correct. The canonical model itself flags this as a known internal inconsistency. Compounding this, the derived figures confirm that the charge arithmetic for EA5 is applied to the wrong fund value: £186.19 at 0.18% and £268.94 at 0.26% both imply 