ion' (i.e. existing Standard Life is more expensive), but the switch charges table (page 11) shows the impact of the recommended alternative as +0.26% (£268.94), meaning the new solution is 0.26% MORE expensive than the Standard Life existing plan — the direction of the cost comparison is directly contradicted within the report itself.","2. Standard Life charge basis error: the derived figures confirm that the EA5 existing annual charge of £186.19 at 0.18% implies a fund value of ~£103,439 (Zurich's value), not Standard Life's current value of £3,002. The charge arithmetic is computed on the wrong fund, making the entire Standard Life cost comparison unreliable.","3. Zurich existing charge inconsistency: page 5 of the report states Zurich's charge as '0.18% fund + £2.12/month admin', implying approximately 0.18% plus fixed admin, but the switch charges table on page 11 states Zurich's current annual recurring charge as 0.93%. These are materially inconsistent and cannot both be correct as a like-for-like total charge, though the derived figures confirm 0.93% at £961.98 implies Zurich's fund value, suggesting 0.93% may be the correct total.","4. New solution ranks lower than existing in projected maturity value: the Defaqto switch report (P6) shows the existing solution projects to £127,000 at medium growth and the new solution to £125,000 — the new solution produces a lower projected maturity value. The report's net benefit claim of 'lower costs' for Standard Life is contradicted by the switch table showing the new solution is more expensive for EA5.","5. Scottish Widows retirement options: the report's comparison table (P21) lists Scottish Widows retirement options as 'Full and partial UFPLS', but the report also states that transferring all pensions into Scottish Widows 'would limit how you can withdraw funds' and that the client 'would be unable to access this pension flexibly' — this may be consistent if the concern is about consolidating everything there, but the encashment rationale relies on flexible access not being available, whereas UFPLS is a form of flexible access.","6. Critical yield mid growth rate: page 11 narrative states 2.94% and page 18 appendix states 2.95% — minor 0.01% discrepancy noted internally.","7. The report states lower costs as a benefit of switching Standard Life, but the switch charges table shows the new solution costs 0.26% more per annum for that plan — the claimed cost advantage is directly contradicted by the report's own table."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The central concern for G9.9 is whether the evidence in the file supports or contradicts the replacement rationale. Several material contradictions exist.\n\nFirst, and most seriously, the Standard Life (EA5) cost rationale is contradicted on multiple levels. The report narrative states the existing Standard Life plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution, which is stated as a reason to switch. However the report's own switch charges table shows the recommended alternative has an impact of +0.26% (£268.94), meaning the new solution is 0.26% MORE expensive than the Standard Life existing plan. The direction is reversed. Furthermore, the derived figures confirm that the £186.19 charge at 0.18% used for EA5 in the switch table implies a fund value of ~£103,439 — Zurich's fund value, not Standard Life's £3,002. The entire Standard Life charge row is arithmetically computed on the wrong fund. The cost justification for the Standard Life switch is therefore both internally contradicted (narrative vs table) and arithmetically wrong. The alternative justifications for the EA5 switch (FAD-only retirement options, lack of adviser management) are present, and P9 supports that Standard Life's options are limited, but the primary cost rationale is unsupported and contradicted.\n\nSecond, for Zurich (EA4), the cost rationale is supported — P3 confirms no drawdown, the switch table shows a 0.49% saving (£504.66), and the derived figures confirm the 0.93% existing charge is computed on Zurich's fund value correctly. However, the charge stated on page 5 (0.18% + £2.12/month admin) is inconsistent with the 0.93% total used in the switch table, creating ambiguity about what the correct existing charge is. The 0.93% figure appears arithmetically correct given Zurich's fund value, so the page 5 figure appears to be an error or partial disclosure.\n\nThird, for Aviva (EA3), P8 confirms lifestyling ('Yes, Lifestage Investment Approach'), which supports the rationale. P8 also confirms no guarantees or GMP. The -0.06% cost advantage is minor but directionally correct.\n\nFourth, for Scottish Widows (EA2), P14 and P10 confirm no ongoing adviser charging and no guarantees, supporting the rationale. However the comparison table in P21 shows Scottish Widows offers 'Full and partial UFPLS', which is a form of flexible access. The report's rationale that the plan 'would limit how you can withdraw funds' applies to the scenario of consolidating everything into Scottish Widows (which only offers UFPLS, not FAD), and the small pots encashment rationale is separately stated. This is internally consistent.\n\nFifth, P6 (Defaqto) shows the new solution ranks 5th with a projected maturity value of £125,000 vs £127,000 for the existing solution at medium growth. The new solution therefore produces a lower projected maturity value. The report claims benefits of lower costs and greater flexibility, but the net benefit for Standard Life switching is contradicted by the charges table.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay: P18 notes an ATR discrepancy (questionnaire result 4, agreed rating 5, and one note references rating 6). This does not directly affect the replacement rationale analysis beyond what is assessed under other checks.\n\nIn summary: the Standard Life switch rationale is directly contradicted by the report's own switch charges table (direction of cost comparison is reversed) and the charges are computed on the wrong fund value. This is a material Data inconsistency going to the heart of the cost justification for EA5. The Zurich charge inconsistency between page 5 and page 11 is also a Data inconsistency. The Aviva and Scottish Widows rationales are broadly supported by evidence. The overall net benefit claim of 'lower costs' is undermined by the Standard Life analysis.","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Does the plan have an auto-switching investment approach? Yes, Lifestage Investment Approach","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"This plan doesn't have any guaranteed rates, including annuity rates","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Guarantees, bonuses, guaranteed minimum pension, safeguarding, life styling, block/bulk transfers, market value reduction, protected or enhanced tax free cash do not apply to this plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Drawdown options are not available on this plan; however, it can be transferred to another provider to do this.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Scottish Widows Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"I can also confirm that there are no guarantees, discounts or bonuses attached to this member's plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Scottish Widows Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Money4Life Plans (Plans starting with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of Servicing Rights or Commission.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["New Solution","0.6%","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing Solution","0.3%","£127,000"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","£186.19","+0.26%","£268.94"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Zurich","0.93%","£961.98","-0.49%","-£504.66 saving"]},{"passageId":"P16","source":"File notes notes - updated.md","category":"C","quote":"Standard Life – Alternative option Discounted as all funds within the same range of 20-60% equities are more expensive than what we can offer at Aviva.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"File notes notes - updated.md","category":"C","quote":"Zurich – Alternative option Zurich do not have risk level 5 funds, a blend of a 7 and a 2 has been used to create the below 5.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Scottish Widows - ZU4225575","Full and partial UFPLS","No","Retain and Encash"]},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - D2301334000","FAD","Yes",""]}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Weak justification"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

